Title:
There and Back Again: An Operators Guide on NTLM Relaying Egress

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
This post documents “There and Back Again,” a tradecraft pattern for coercing NTLM authentication to egress to the internet, capturing it on a cloud VM, and relaying it back into the target environment to compromise internal services. It addresses common operator constraints in “ceded access” engagements where you lack L2 positioning or local admin to bind to 445/TCP for classic SMB relay, and where firewall rules block direct WebDAV-to-LDAP relay paths. The technique uses an internet-facing relay host (e.g., Azure VM) with port redirection and SSH reverse port forwarding, plus a SOCKS5 path through the C2/teamserver to reach internal LDAP/LDAPS/ADCS targets. The walkthrough focuses on WebDAV coercion over 443/TCP (to bypass SMB egress controls), relaying to LDAPS to perform Shadow Credentials (msDS-KeyCredentialLink) writes, then using PKINIT + S4U2Self to obtain admin-equivalent Kerberos tickets for local/remote takeover. It also covers practical prerequisites (DNS record creation, starting EFS/WebClient services) and operational caveats (attributable NTLM metadata traversing the internet). Defensive guidance emphasizes LDAP signing/channel binding, EPA on ADCS web enrollment, and strict egress/inbound firewalling.

Technical Focus:
- NTLM coercion + relay via egress (SMB/WebDAV) and cloud redirectors
- SSH reverse port forwarding, iptables port redirection, SOCKS5/proxychains pivoting
- WebClient/WebDAV coercion and EFSRPC (PetitPotam) triggering
- NTLM relay to LDAP/LDAPS and Shadow Credentials (msDS-KeyCredentialLink)
- PKINIT, Kerberos TGT acquisition, S4U2Self delegation, ticket-based lateral movement
- ADCS web enrollment relay considerations (ESC8 / EPA)

Use Cases:
- Relaying coerced NTLM when you cannot bind 445 locally (no local admin) during C2-only ops
- Bypassing SMB egress restrictions by coercing WebDAV over 443 and relaying to LDAP/LDAPS
- Local privilege escalation on a compromised workstation via LDAPS shadow-credential write + Kerberos delegation
- Identity/computer account takeover leading to follow-on AD compromise paths
- Validating real-world impact of missing LDAP relay protections or ADCS EPA in mature networks

Keywords:
NTLM relay, NTLM egress, WebDAV, WebClient service, SMB, port 445, port 443, PetitPotam, EFSRPC, LDAP relay, LDAPS, LDAP signing, channel binding token (CBT), Extended Protection for Authentication (EPA), ADCS web enrollment, ESC8, ntlmrelayx, Impacket, proxychains, SOCKS5, SSH reverse port forward, iptables REDIRECT, Shadow Credentials, msDS-KeyCredentialLink, PKINIT, Kerberos TGT, S4U2Self, Certipy, dnstool, krbrelayx, Credential Guard, WMIExec